# Binary assets — never normalise line endings or diff.
# Text files keep the repository's existing checkout behaviour (core.autocrlf);
# the web test helpers normalise CRLF->LF at their read point, so they pass on
# both a CRLF (windows CI) and an LF checkout. Do not add a global text/eol rule:
# the committed blobs of the help/guide content are LF (core.eol=lf), and its tests
# assert that against the git object, not against the checkout, so the assertions
# hold on any shape; a text/eol rule now would store nothing different and would
# make a repository-wide renormalisation the next required step.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.eot binary
*.otf binary
*.zip binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.wasm binary
*.pdf binary
*.bin binary
*.dat binary
# Web build (BL-031). These are scoped rules, not a global text/eol rule: every blob below is already LF
# in git (git ls-files --eol shows i/lf), so nothing is renormalised and no tracked file changes.
# Inputs the build reads as text: always LF on checkout, so a CRLF checkout (autocrlf=true, the windows
# runner) builds the same bytes as an LF one. docs/user/*.md is read by the help pages (?raw).
/web/index.html text eol=lf
/web/setup/index.html text eol=lf
/web/lite/index.html text eol=lf
/docs/user/*.md text eol=lf
# The favicon is copied into the build as a file (its name carries a hash of its bytes), so a CRLF checkout
# would build a different file name than an LF one. Found on the windows runner by check-dist (rc promotion, Nov 2026).
/web/src/brand/favicon.svg text eol=lf
# Built output: byte for byte on any checkout, never converted.
/web/dist/** -text
/cmd/unravis-setup/ui/** -text
/cmd/unravis-lite/ui/** -text
# Shell scripts run on Linux runners and in Git Bash: a CRLF checkout breaks them (a carriage return ends up in the shebang line). Scoped, like the rules above; blobs are already LF.
/tools/ci/*.sh text eol=lf
